I think what it comes down to is you have to not know what went wrong or 
what you need to do next. I was trying to think of a way to be stuck and 
I thought maybe if you lost an invocation artifact under Medusa's water 
(without knowing where), AND it was covered by other items, AND you had 
no means of magical breathing or polycontrol, AND you can't cast cone of 
cold and are low on cold wands, it would be a real pain to retrieve the 
artifact. But even then, at least you'd know exactly what you have to do 
and there would be an end in sight.

So to quit, you'd have to really be wondering what's next to do. What if 
you're just playing along, and you suddenly realize you don't know what 
happened to the Bell of Opening? Suppose further that you did the quest 
before anything else. Then, the bell may be on any level, possibly 
carried by a nymph or stored under a big stash. In particular if I 
hadn't played the game in a while and kept poor notes, the game would 
start looking hopeless.

One other thing would be if you reached a very high level, so powerful 
monsters were generated even early on, and then had all of your items 
destroyed (say, BoH accident followed by black dragon accident). The 
game would be looking bad because it'd be so hard to work your way back 
to nice items.

Early on is much more likely for me to abandon a game. If I fall down 10 
dlvls through a trapdoor before the 100th move, I'll probably quit (or 
more likely die). By the time you get the Amulet you have to have 
something spectacularly aggrivating happen to you to make it not worth 
remeding.

